@*
    What happened to one work item, in one column, newest first.

    Section 71 asks for an activity timeline and the page it belongs on already had every fact in
    four separate tables — commits, pull requests, builds, deployments — with the comments below
    them and the moves nowhere at all. Four tables side by side is not a timeline: it makes the
    reader hold four clocks and interleave them, which is the work this component does once.

    Presentation only. No @rendermode, no @inject, no query and no form: it takes finished sentences
    and draws them. The wording is decided in Streamed, where the facts are read, so that the
    sentences can be tested without a browser and this file cannot quietly start deciding what a
    status change means.

    Deliberately in Components/Pages/Work, the same folder and therefore the same namespace as
    Item.razor. _Imports.razor reaches only Components and Components.Layout, and Razor's answer to
    a tag it cannot resolve is to emit it as unknown HTML that the browser silently drops — which is
    how the client page's whole attachments section was a heading with nothing under it for weeks.
*@

<section class="standing">
    <h2>What has happened</h2>

    @if (Lines.Count == 0)
    {
        <p class="empty">
            Nothing has been recorded against this work yet. Moving it, saying something on the
            thread, or pushing a commit that names its reference all put a line here.
        </p>
    }
    else
    {
        @foreach (var day in Streamed.ByDay(Lines))
        {
            <h3>@Day(day.Key)</h3>

            @foreach (var line in day)
            {
                <article class="entry">
                    @*
                        The time inside an element rather than beside the pill. Razor emits nothing
                        between an @expression and the element after it, so a bare time followed by
                        a chip renders as "13:32Merged" — the fault CLAUDE.md records in thirty-six
                        places, and the reason the incident timeline wraps its own time in a strong.
                    *@
                    <div class="entry__head">
                        <strong>@line.At.ToLocalTime().ToString("HH:mm")</strong>

                        @if (line.Tone is { } tone)
                        {
                            <span class="pill @tone">@line.ToneSaid</span>
                        }
                    </div>

                    <p class="entry__subject">
                        @line.Said

                        @if (line.Link is { } href)
                        {
                            <a href="@href">@(line.LinkSaid ?? "Look")</a>
                        }
                    </p>
                </article>
            }
        }
    }

    @if (Withheld is { } missing)
    {
        <p class="subtle">@missing</p>
    }

    @if (Cut is { } older)
    {
        <p class="subtle">@older</p>
    }

    @if (TrailHref is { } trail)
    {
        <p class="subtle"><a href="@trail">Every change to this, in the trail</a></p>
    }
</section>

@code {
    /// <summary>The lines to draw, already in the order they should be read.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Ordered by the caller rather than here, because ordering is a decision about instants — a
    /// commit from Nairobi carries +03:00 — and it belongs with the code that knows what the
    /// timestamps mean. This component's job is to draw what it is given.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public IReadOnlyList<StreamLine> Lines { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>What this reader is not being shown, and why. Null when they see everything.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Withheld { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What was left off for length. Null when nothing was.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Cut { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Where the full change trail is, for a reader allowed to read it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? TrailHref { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A day, as a heading.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// "Today" and "Yesterday" by name because those are the two days somebody scanning a stream is
    /// actually placing things against, and a date they have to work out is a date they skip. The
    /// year is kept on everything older: a stream is read months later and "4 March" in January is
    /// ambiguous in the direction that matters.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string Day(DateOnly day)
    {
        var today = DateOnly.FromDateTime(DateTime.Now);

        return day == today ? "Today"
            : day == today.AddDays(-1) ? "Yesterday"
            : day.ToString("d MMMM yyyy");
    }
}
